maya: also include a short non-technical "Why ephemeral keys" blurb (blast radius + easier rotation) and a security note about not logging tokens. I'll coordinate wording with security.

rene: draft tiny rotation helper for docs-only (very compact):
```js
// docs/snippets/rotate-docs.js
function startRotation(getToken, applyKey) {
  let stopped = false;
  async function run() {
    if (stopped) return;
    const tok = await getToken();
    applyKey(tok);
    setTimeout(run, 50 * 60 * 1000); // refresh before 1h
  }
  run();
  return { stop: () => { stopped = true; } };
}
module.exports = { startRotation };
```

sam: nice and tiny. for the sample app return the stop handle and add a short comment: "do not log token value". Also change snippets/auth.md to show client init + setApiKey rather than inline env in constructor so rotation pattern is obvious.

pete: add a compact GH Actions snippet that mints ephemeral token and writes to GITHUB_ENV, then uses it in the deploy step. ops asked for a 1-line migration note for Dedicated/private customers (use KMS-signed mint requests) and link to infra runbook.

rene: will add GH Actions snippet and migration blurb. draft GH Actions example (docs-only):
```yaml
- name: Mint ephemeral key
  run: |
    curl -sS -X POST "$MINT_URL" -H "Authorization: Bearer $JOB_TOKEN" -d '{"purpose":"docs-demo"}' \
      | jq -r .token >> "$GITHUB_ENV"
- name: Deploy using ephemeral key
  env:
    REDWOOD_API_KEY: ${{ env.REDWOOD_API_KEY }}
  run: node deploy.js
```

maya: small nit: show how to write the env var correctly (e.g., echo "REDWOOD_API_KEY=$(jq -r .token)" >> $GITHUB_ENV) or use the safe pattern. I can add the exact line.

